-- Four public historical answers. The DO statement is atomic and safe to retry.
DO $ask$
DECLARE
  item jsonb;
  current_hash text;
  result_hash text;
BEGIN
  FOR item IN SELECT value FROM jsonb_array_elements($answers$[{"id":"44b4100a-aca5-43d8-86e7-6b37a154ed9d","old":"8a51ee6385fc43c4e3e74ebdedd63a1c8a32e6437bcdd1cf8359adc3a6a28e25","new":"499240c631115001214555f6755812a91a57cecdc5b177699e846d84691208ec","text":"In the context of trauma and damage control resuscitation, targets for coagulation and platelets are managed through **haemostatic resuscitation** and point-of-care testing.\n\n*   **Platelets.** In ongoing traumatic bleeding, the European guideline suggests maintaining a platelet count above 50 × 10⁹/L, or above 100 × 10⁹/L with traumatic brain injury. NICE distinguishes severe or critical-site bleeding, where higher thresholds up to 100 × 10⁹/L may be appropriate, from surgery involving a critical site, such as the posterior segment of the eye, where a count above 100 × 10⁹/L can be considered. Eye involvement alone does not determine the transfusion threshold.\n*   **Initial components.** For adult major traumatic bleeding, NICE recommends starting with one unit of plasma per unit of red cells and changing to laboratory-guided treatment as early as possible. Reassess platelets, fibrinogen, clotting tests, the rate of bleeding and, where available, validated viscoelastic findings. Treat major bleeding with a functional fibrinogen deficit or Clauss fibrinogen ≤1.5 g/L with fibrinogen concentrate or cryoprecipitate under the local protocol. Do not use an isolated INR as a universal endpoint.\n\nActivate the local **major haemorrhage protocol** promptly when major ongoing bleeding is suspected, using the patient’s haemodynamic state, trajectory and response to initial resuscitation. Transfusion demand and base deficit can support assessment, but do not wait for a numerical blood-volume threshold or use a single base-deficit result as a stand-alone decision rule. Once available, **viscoelastic testing (TEG/ROTEM)** should be used to guide individual component therapy rather than relying solely on fixed ratios.\n\n**Read more on AnaesthesiaCore**\n- [Trauma & Emergency Anaesthesia](/clinical/trauma-emergency) — details on major haemorrhage protocol activation and markers of occult haemorrhage.\n- [Haematological Co-Existing Disease](/perioperative/haematological-disease) — provides specific platelet targets for CNS injuries and general bleeding.\n- [Mass Casualty, CRM & Military Anaesthesia](/clinical/mass-casualty-military) — outlines the pillars of damage-control resuscitation and early TXA use.\n\n**Sources**\n\n- [NICE NG39, major trauma and blood components](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng39/chapter/recommendations)\n- [NICE NG24, platelet transfusion and critical-site procedures](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng24/resources/blood-transfusion-pdf-1837331897029)\n- [European major bleeding guideline, recommendations 29–30](https://doi.org/10.1186/s13054-023-04327-7)"},{"id":"97570c5d-61cd-477b-af59-ec4f5f1f6a5f","old":"f975735f57f741f74410b6bf077fd4148b61fa00d29603ecf327bf04f2c149de","new":"a42dec085bb6396a058350efd24fbde7026daca2641332fc8eef0554c2704f8c","text":"Management of the trauma patient focuses on **damage control resuscitation (DCR)**, which aims to address the 'lethal triad' of hypothermia, acidosis, and coagulopathy.\n\n### Haemodynamic Targets\nIn an adult with uncontrolled bleeding and no evidence of brain injury, the European trauma guideline recommends an initial systolic pressure of 80–90 mmHg (MAP 50–60 mmHg) until haemorrhage control. Reassess organ perfusion and proceed to definitive control urgently.\n*   **Haemorrhagic shock with TBI:** NICE advises matching fluid resuscitation to the dominant threat. Use a restrictive approach if bleeding predominates and a less restrictive approach if brain injury predominates, to preserve cerebral perfusion. The European guideline recommends MAP at least 80 mmHg specifically for severe TBI (GCS ≤8). An age-specific systolic goal may also be considered using the Brain Trauma Foundation severe TBI guideline.\n*   **Monitored severe TBI:** The Brain Trauma Foundation recommends treating ICP above 22 mmHg and targeting CPP 60–70 mmHg, using the clinical picture and autoregulation to individualise treatment.\n\n### Haemostatic and Metabolic Targets\nIn adults with major traumatic bleeding, activate the local major haemorrhage protocol. NICE recommends an initial plasma-to-red-cell ratio of 1:1 and changing to laboratory-guided treatment as early as possible. Validated viscoelastic testing can inform that assessment.\n*   **Tranexamic Acid (TXA):** Give `1 g` IV bolus over 10 minutes followed by a `1 g` infusion over 8 hours. This must be started within **3 hours** of injury (CRASH-2).\n*   **Temperature:** Maintain normothermia (`>36°C`) with active rewarming.\n*   **Biochemical:** Avoid hyperglycaemia and hyponatraemia.\n*   **Oxygenation:** Target **SpO₂ >94%**.\n*   **Ventilation:** Aim for normocapnia, using arterial gases and injury-specific targets. Avoid routine hyperventilation; temporary hypocapnia is a rescue measure for imminent cerebral herniation while definitive treatment is arranged.\n\n### Induction Targets\nChoose and dose the induction agent for the patient’s physiology, injury and available expertise. Ketamine and etomidate are options; neither guarantees haemodynamic stability. Reduce or titrate doses in severe shock and prepare haemodynamic support. Propofol and thiopentone can cause marked hypotension in hypovolaemia. Do not teach ketamine as universally superior in trauma: the large 2025 ketamine-versus-etomidate trial excluded primary trauma and found more peri-intubation cardiovascular collapse with ketamine in its critically ill population.\n\n**Read more on AnaesthesiaCore**\n- [Trauma & Emergency Anaesthesia](/clinical/trauma-emergency) — details on damage control resuscitation, permissive hypotension, and TBI-specific targets.\n- [Neurointensive Care](/intensive-care/neurointensive-care) — specific ICP and CPP targets and the BTF 2016 guidelines.\n- [Neuroanaesthesia](/clinical/neuroanaesthesia) — explains the calculation and management of cerebral perfusion pressure.\n- [Mass Casualty, CRM & Military Anaesthesia](/clinical/mass-casualty-military) — provides additional context on damage-control resuscitation.\n\n**Sources**\n\n- [NICE NG39, fluid resuscitation and blood components](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng39/chapter/recommendations)\n- [European major bleeding guideline, recommendations 13 and 23](https://doi.org/10.1186/s13054-023-04327-7)\n- [Brain Trauma Foundation, severe TBI guidelines, sections 15–17](https://braintrauma.org/coma/guidelines/severe-tbi)\n- [Ketamine versus etomidate in critically ill adults](https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMoa2511420)"},{"id":"f1805abd-dbab-4273-b519-627fa42051f7","old":"a50aeb7326971b74a8f1ad48e6411d89188ee0c3ab2351f08dd08c488ef01aca","new":"b1be741a6a311472c4bfc8b8c972243a7671984882bb9b7f81c58dbd85a067d7","text":"In the Phase 0/I–III convention for a time capnogram, Phase 0 is inspiration and Phases I–III are expiration.\n\n*   **Phase I (early expiratory baseline).** Gas initially exhaled from conducting-airway dead space reaches the sensor. In the absence of rebreathing, its CO₂ is normally close to zero.\n*   **Phase II (Expiratory Upstroke):** A rapid, steep rise in CO₂ concentration as the dead space gas is replaced by a mixture of dead space and alveolar gas.\n*   **Phase III (Alveolar Plateau):** A gently rising plateau during predominantly alveolar emptying. The CO₂ concentration at the very end of this phase is the **ETCO₂** (End-Tidal CO₂), typically `4.7–6.0 kPa` (`35–45 mmHg`) in healthy adults.\n*   **Phase 0 (Inspiratory Downstroke):** A near-vertical drop back to the zero baseline as the patient begins to inhale CO₂-free inspiratory gas.\n\n**Key Clinical Angles:**\n*   **Alpha Angle:** The angle between Phase II and Phase III. This widens (becomes more obtuse) in obstructive conditions like asthma or COPD, creating a \"shark-fin\" appearance.\n*   **Beta Angle:** The angle between Phase III and Phase 0. A raised inspiratory CO₂ baseline suggests rebreathing. Interpret the angle with the rest of the waveform and the circuit.\n\n**Read more on AnaesthesiaCore**\n- [Capnography](/physics/capnography) — detailed breakdown of waveform phases, angles, and abnormal patterns like curare clefts.\n- [Humidity & Gas Analysis](/physics/humidity-gas-sampling) — compares side-stream and main-stream sampling methods for CO₂ monitoring.\n\n**Source**\n\n- [Bhavani-Shankar and Philip, defining segments and phases of a time capnogram](https://doi.org/10.1097/00000539-200010000-00038)"},{"id":"f65d99f0-1315-4ff1-93be-37935f9ea24b","old":"f2f4043c83029c5b0b86415e7987e2fa835ab8c2d22c38561205afa598695447","new":"bcfaa173b58a686a195e5990f0703746e585f1f0a964d534322c864421f4fb0e","text":"Intraoperative transfusion requires separate decisions for stable anaemia and active major bleeding. Assess the cause and severity, use patient-specific transfusion thresholds, and activate the **major haemorrhage protocol** promptly when life-threatening bleeding is suspected.\n\n### 1. Triggering and Ratios\nActivate the local major haemorrhage protocol promptly for suspected life-threatening bleeding, using physiology, ongoing loss and response to resuscitation. Unit-count definitions must not delay activation. Stable perioperative anaemia requires a separate transfusion decision.\n*   **Empirical Ratios:** For active major haemorrhage, use the local initial component pack and transition early to laboratory and/or validated viscoelastic guidance. A 1:1:1 approach is used in some trauma protocols; it is not a requirement for every intraoperative transfusion.\n*   **Tranexamic Acid (TXA):** Use the regimen appropriate to the bleeding context and operation. For bleeding trauma, give 1 g IV over 10 minutes followed by 1 g over 8 hours, started as early as possible within 3 hours of injury. This injury-based window is not a universal rule for all surgical bleeding.\n\n### 2. Physiological & Laboratory Targets\nDuring active major haemorrhage, use repeated laboratory tests and/or locally validated TEG/ROTEM algorithms alongside clinical assessment. The appropriate targets depend on bleeding, operation and patient factors:\n*   **Haemoglobin:** During active major bleeding, guide transfusion by physiology, blood loss and repeated results. Once bleeding is controlled, many stable patients use a restrictive threshold of 70 g/L and a post-transfusion target of 70–90 g/L; acute coronary syndromes and other special settings require individual targets.\n*   **Platelets:** Common procedural thresholds are >50 ×10⁹/L, with higher targets for selected high-risk procedures; >100 ×10⁹/L applies to critical sites such as the CNS and posterior segment of the eye, not every eye operation.\n*   **Fibrinogen.** In major traumatic bleeding, replace fibrinogen when a validated viscoelastic test indicates functional deficiency or Clauss fibrinogen is ≤1.5 g/L, using fibrinogen concentrate or cryoprecipitate under the local protocol. Other operative settings have different triggers.\n*   **Coagulation.** During liver transplantation, use the stage of the operation, observed diffuse coagulopathic bleeding and the local validated algorithm to interpret viscoelastic results. TEG reports reaction time (R) and ROTEM reports clotting time (CT). A prolonged value or isolated INR does not by itself indicate plasma. Select treatment according to the cause and the full trace, then reassess.\n\n### 3. Monitoring & Complications\nDuring a major haemorrhage protocol, reassess blood gases, electrolytes and acid–base status frequently, with timing guided by the rate of bleeding, transfusion and clinical change. \n*   **Citrate Toxicity:** Citrate in transfused blood can reduce ionised calcium. During major traumatic bleeding, measure ionised calcium repeatedly and correct hypocalcaemia towards the normal range (approximately 1.1–1.3 mmol/L), using calcium chloride according to the local protocol and repeat measurements.\n*   **Hyperkalaemia:** Stored PRBCs can contribute to hyperkalaemia. Monitor potassium and ECG. Insulin/glucose shifts potassium into cells; IV calcium treats cardiac toxicity but does not lower potassium. Use the appropriate hyperkalaemia protocol and arrange potassium removal when indicated.\n*   **Temperature:** Maintain core temperature `>36°C` using warmed fluids and forced-air warming, as hypothermia exacerbates coagulopathy.\n*   **Metabolic:** Aim for a `pH >7.2`.\n\n### 4. Special Considerations\nIn **liver surgery**, the coagulopathy is \"rebalanced\" (decreased pro- and anti-coagulants). Avoid over-transfusion to minimize portal pressure, but be prepared for post-reperfusion hyperfibrinolysis. In **emergency surgery with thrombocytopenia**, assess neuraxial and invasive-access risks individually, considering count, trend, cause, other coagulation abnormalities and urgency. Obtain necessary resuscitation access safely; thrombocytopenia alone does not prohibit all invasive lines.\n\n**Read more on AnaesthesiaCore**\n- [Trauma & Emergency Anaesthesia](/clinical/trauma-emergency) — MTP activation triggers and complications of massive transfusion.\n- [Transfusion & Coagulation](/intensive-care/transfusion-coagulation) — Evidence-based ratios (PROPPR trial) and guidance on TXA.\n- [Hepatobiliary & Transplant Anaesthesia](/clinical/hepatobiliary-transplant) — Managing rebalanced coagulopathy and TEG/ROTEM-guided therapy.\n- [Haematology & Immunity](/physiology/haematology-immunity) — Specific physiological targets for fibrinogen, platelets, and calcium.\n\n**Sources**\n\n- [NICE NG39, major trauma](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng39/chapter/recommendations)\n- [NICE NG24, blood transfusion](https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng24)\n- [European major bleeding guideline, recommendations 29–31](https://doi.org/10.1186/s13054-023-04327-7)\n- [Liver transplantation haemostatic management consensus, recommendations 3–4 and table 2](https://doi.org/10.1097/TP.0000000000004014)\n- [RCUK, special circumstances](https://www.resus.org.uk/professional-library/2025-resuscitation-guidelines/special-circumstances-guidelines)"}]$answers$::jsonb) ORDER BY value->>'id'
  LOOP
    IF encode(sha256(convert_to(item->>'text','UTF8')),'hex') <> item->>'new' THEN
      RAISE EXCEPTION 'Invalid target payload for %', item->>'id';
    END IF;
    SELECT encode(sha256(convert_to(answer,'UTF8')),'hex') INTO STRICT current_hash
      FROM public.ask_qa_library WHERE id=(item->>'id')::uuid FOR UPDATE;
    IF current_hash = item->>'new' THEN CONTINUE; END IF;
    IF current_hash <> item->>'old' THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Historical answer % changed', item->>'id'; END IF;
    UPDATE public.ask_qa_library SET answer=item->>'text' WHERE id=(item->>'id')::uuid
      RETURNING encode(sha256(convert_to(answer,'UTF8')),'hex') INTO STRICT result_hash;
    IF result_hash <> item->>'new' THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'Historical answer % failed verification', item->>'id'; END IF;
  END LOOP;
END;
$ask$;
